  "body": {
    "id": "thm:outer-fiber-confidence",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "The outward inverse satisfies \\(\\Pr_P\\{\\mathcal D(P)\\subseteq\\mathcal D_n\\}\\ge1-\\eta\\) uniformly over \\(\\mathcal M_d^\\circ\\). If the reporting rule outputs no arrow when \\(\\mathcal D_n=\\varnothing\\) and otherwise reports only arrows present with the same orientation in every \\(G\\in\\mathcal D_n\\), then the unconditional probability that it reports any arrow not compelled throughout \\(\\mathcal D(P)\\) is at most \\(\\eta\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(P\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal D(P)\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal D_n\\)",
      "\\(\\eta\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal M_d^\\circ\\)",
      "\\(G\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:degree-four-completeness",
      "thm:coefficient-confidence",
      "def:outer-fiber",
      "def:observational-fiber"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Let\n\\[\n \\mathcal E_n:=\\{t_4(P)\\in C_n\\}.\n\\]\nThe coefficient-confidence theorem gives \\(\\Pr_P(\\mathcal E_n)\\ge1-\\eta\\), uniformly over the strict model.  On \\(\\mathcal E_n\\), take any \\(G\\in\\mathcal D(P)\\).  By the definition of \\(\\mathcal D(P)\\), there is a strict parameter point on \\(G\\) inducing \\(P\\).  Hence \\(G\\in\\mathcal D(t_4(P))\\).  Since \\(t_4(P)\\in C_n\\), the definition of the outward inverse gives\n\\[\n G\\in\\mathcal D(t_4(P))\n \\subseteq\\bigcup_{t\\in C_n}\\mathcal D(t)=\\mathcal D_n.\n\\]\nThis holds for every \\(G\\in\\mathcal D(P)\\), so\n\\[\n \\Pr_P\\{\\mathcal D(P)\\subseteq\\mathcal D_n\\}\n \\ge\\Pr_P(\\mathcal E_n)\\ge1-\\eta.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nDefine the reporting rule to output no arrow when \\(\\mathcal D_n=\\varnothing\\), and otherwise to output only an arrow that occurs with the same orientation in every \\(G\\in\\mathcal D_n\\).  If (13)'s inclusion event holds, every reported arrow occurs with that orientation in every member of the subset \\(\\mathcal D(P)\\); therefore it is genuinely compelled throughout the population fiber.  Thus\n\\[\n \\{\\text{at least one falsely compelled arrow is reported}\\}\n \\subseteq\n \\{\\mathcal D(P)\\nsubseteq\\mathcal D_n\\},\n\\]\nand (13) proves that the probability of any false compulsion is at most \\(\\eta\\), uniformly over \\(\\mathcal M_d^\\circ\\).  This is an unconditional familywise statement for a rule that acts only when the outer set is nonempty; division by \\(\\Pr_P\\{\\mathcal D_n\\ne\\varnothing\\}\\) is neither used nor justified.",
    "justification": "Inference targets the full non-singleton population fiber and therefore avoids false certainty from graph-then-report workflows.",
    "gap": "Strieder et al. invert tests for linear-model effects and Wang et al. give asymptotic ordering sets; neither covers every graph in a strict PB-SCM population fiber with finite-sample probability.",
    "consumer": "The football-event and shopping-mall analyses can report only causal arrows supported simultaneously across structural and sampling uncertainty.",
